ead of a select few independent  surgical abortion clinics this improved  access and increased medical safety but  since then four more similar clinics  have opened and they receive no such  support from The Province Omar's is one  of them I don't really understand or  know how they've come to the conclusion  that these four clinics deserve funding  while other clinics don't abortions are  covered in Ontario with the province  reimbursing doctors for insured care but  Omar says that money is not enough to  cover all of the expenses needed to keep  his Clinic going we have to pay for  medication out of pocket we do pay for  rent we also do pay the salary of all  the nurses and counselors and  secretaries that we have working for us  on top of this there are Services his  Clinic offers that Ontario doesn't cover  including running a 24-hour patient  hotline so to maintain retain these  Services he relies on his patients to  pay a fee but only if they can a lot of  times we'd actually rather see it as a  donation with the specific demographic  that we deal with a lot of the patients  are not actually able to pay how much we  charge which is uh sixty dollars it  speaks to the commitment of those who  are providing the service because  they're doing it because they're meeting  a need they're doing it even though  they're being financially stretched to  to to to provide it Omar says his Clinic  has asked Ontario for funding twice  no response either time no just for the  the first time they said that our  requests would be forwarded to the  people that make these decisions and  then we never got any follow-up as a  result in an email Global News asked  Ontario's Ministry of Health for a  response to Omar's claims and why  clinics like his are excluded from its  funding Model A Ministry spokesperson  replied in a statement but never  answered these questions what would  happen to abortion access if clinics  like yours were forced to shut down oh  it'd be a huge blow if if 